WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:32.460
<v Ira Glass>We like to believe that there are laws among thieves. We like to believe that there are rules of honor, that when Michael Corleone orders a mob hit, it's because some code has been violated, that if Tony Soprano chokes a guy to death in a parking lot, it is because that guy broke some rule, that he deserved it somehow. This is one of the most romantic ideas that we have about crime, that there could be a system, that there could be ancient, strict rules, and that there are people out there who actually try to follow those rules, you know?
<v Ira Glass>我们愿意相信盗亦有道。我们愿意相信存在某种荣誉准则，认为当迈克尔·柯里昂下令帮派谋杀时，那是因为某种准则被触犯了；认为当托尼·索普拉诺在停车场勒死一个人时，是因为那个人破坏了某种规矩，他罪有应得。这是我们对犯罪最浪漫的构想之一：即犯罪可能存在一套体系，存在古老而严格的规则，并且真的有人在尝试遵循这些规则，你知道吗？

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:53.930
<v Ira Glass>And at one level, of course we want to believe that. Of course we want to believe that it's all so orderly, rather than the thing that we fear about crime, which is that it's random and can strike us at any time, that it's senseless. If there's a system, if there are rules, if it's a business, it doesn't even seem like crime.
<v Ira Glass>在某种程度上，我们当然愿意相信这一点。我们当然愿意相信这一切都是如此井然有序，而不是我们对犯罪最恐惧的那面——即犯罪是随机的，随时可能降临到我们头上，它是毫无意义的。如果存在一套体系，如果存在规矩，如果它是一门生意，那它看起来甚至就不像是犯罪了。

00:00:53.930 --> 00:01:30.460
<v Ira Glass>Well, it turns out that there's a classic American primer about the laws of pimps and pimping written in 1968, Iceberg Slim's Pimp: The Story of My Life . In it, Iceberg Slim explains the rules of being a successful pimp. It is such a clearly-defined tradition, the way he tells it, that at one point, he visits an older pimp for advice. And the guy doesn't just give him the advice. He begins by laying out the entire history of pimping in the Americas. And then he gives him the pointers on how to pimp by the book.
<v Ira Glass>事实证明，1968年出版的一本关于皮条客和拉皮条法则的经典美国入门书——冰山斯利姆（Iceberg Slim）所著的《皮条客：我的一生》——解释了成为一名成功皮条客的规则。他所讲述的这种传统有着非常明确的定义，以至于有一次，他去请教一位老皮条客。而那个人不仅给了他建议，还先为他梳理了美洲拉皮条行业的全部历史。然后，他才向他传授了如何按规矩拉皮条的诀窍。

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:54.410
<v Ira Glass>"By the book" is always said in this reverential way when you read Iceberg Slim. "By the book" means, when it comes to pimping, knowing that you're using people, outmaneuvering them when they try to play you, and never getting sentimental about it. It's business.
<v Ira Glass>当你读冰山斯利姆的书时，“按规矩”总是以一种虔诚的方式被提及。在拉皮条的语境下，“按规矩”意味着：你知道自己在利用别人，在她们试图算计你时胜过她们，并且永远不要对此产生感情。这就是生意。

00:01:54.410 --> 00:02:17.870
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, a former pimp makes the case that in Oakland, California, in the 1970s, in the last heyday of American pimps in a stronghold of American pimping, the rules of the game that Iceberg Slim describes were not just a reassuring fiction but a way of life for hundreds of people, maybe thousands.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，一位前皮条客提出，在20世纪70年代的加利福尼亚州奥克兰市——那个美国皮条客鼎盛时期的重镇——冰山斯利姆所描述的游戏规则不仅仅是一种让人安心的虚构，而是成百上千，甚至上万人的一种生活方式。

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:42.150
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Most weeks on our program, we choose a theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today, instead, we are devoting our entire program to just one story of a man who tried to be a pimp, found out he did not have the stomach for the violence, and how that let to his downfall in the pimping world.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ电台和公共广播国际的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们节目都会设定一个主题，为你带来与该主题相关的各种故事。但今天，我们将整个节目献给一个男人的故事：他曾试图成为一名皮条客，却发现自己受不了其中的暴力，而这也导致了他后来在皮条客世界中的没落。

00:02:42.150 --> 00:03:11.310
<v Ira Glass>Today's show is the story of him and his friends and his observations about the rules of the game on the street back then. I have to say, part of what's amazing about him is the detail that he gets to in describing how that world worked. Our program today, Pimp Anthropology. Act One, Rules of the Game. Act Two, The Price of Ignoring the Rules. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目讲述了他和他的朋友们，以及他对当时街头游戏规则的观察。我必须说，他令人惊叹的地方之一，在于他描述那个世界运作方式时所展现的细节。我们今天的节目名为《皮条客人类学》。第一幕：游戏规则。第二幕：无视规则的代价。请继续收听。

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:33.340
<v Ira Glass>The guy who is the subject of our show today spoke with Tamar Brott, a writer in California who he's known for years. I should say before we begin that if you are listening with small children, there is no sex in today's show. None at all. No graphic descriptions, no sex acts are even named, though there is a scene or two where men hit women. Well, here's Tamar Brott.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们节目的主角与加利福尼亚州的作家塔玛·布罗特进行了交谈，他们相识多年。在开始之前我得说明，如果您带着年幼的孩子收听，今天的节目中没有性描写。完全没有。没有露骨的描述，甚至没有提及性行为，尽管有一两个男人殴打女人的场景。好的，下面请听塔玛·布罗特的讲述。

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:45.210
<v Tamar Brott>When Kevin was growing up in Oakland, he and his childhood friends Mark, Keith, and Little Ricky decided they were going to be pimps. And that's what they became. This was in the '70s, and the city was overrun with pimps.
<v Tamar Brott>当凯文在奥克兰长大时，他和儿时好友马克、基思和小瑞奇决定要成为皮条客。他们后来也确实走上了这条路。那是在70年代，城市里到处都是皮条客。

00:03:45.210 --> 00:04:24.280
<v Tamar Brott>The vice cops say that Oakland had the largest prostitution trade in all of California. The infrastructure was perfect-- plenty of freeway access to cheap motels, and lots of places for the pimps to shop. They went to Hill's Shoes for their footwear, Olsen's Cadillac for their custom cars, and Wilson's House of Leather for their clothes. At the height of his glory, Kevin wore a gold lame suit with beaver fur collar and cuffs. Mark favored a long, white, leather coat. Keith was partial to jewel-encrusted dollar signs. This is their story.
<v Tamar Brott>风化案警察说，奥克兰拥有整个加利福尼亚州最大的卖淫交易。基础设施非常完美——有通往廉价汽车旅馆的高速公路，还有很多皮条客消费的地方。他们去希尔鞋店买鞋，去奥尔森凯迪拉克买定制车，去威尔逊皮革店买衣服。在凯文最风光的时候，他穿着金色的金属丝西装，领口和袖口饰有海狸皮毛。马克偏爱长款白色皮大衣。基思则喜欢珠宝镶嵌的美元符号。这就是他们的故事。

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:43.250
<v Kevin>Where I was raised, a lot of kids, we looked at role models. Just as they do today, look at athletes today. Well, back then, one of the first images of success that you would see were Cadillacs. That was a sign of success for people in poor and blighted areas, especially in housing projects.
<v Kevin>在我长大的地方，很多孩子都会寻找榜样。就像他们今天看待运动员一样。嗯，在当时，你看到的第一批成功形象就是凯迪拉克。对于贫困和落后地区的人们来说，这是一种成功的标志，特别是在廉价住房区。

00:04:43.250 --> 00:05:07.350
<v Kevin>One of my earliest memories was of a guy named Robert Charles. He was a local guy who had, at the time, what I thought was the greatest car in the world. He had a Cadillac that probably was a '60, '61 Cadillac. Brown. The car was beautiful. And he would ride through the neighborhood. And I didn't realize at the time that he was a pimp. But he'd ride through the neighborhood. He had the girls.
<v Kevin>我最早的记忆之一是一个叫罗伯特·查尔斯的人。他是当地的一个人，在那时，他拥有我当时认为世界上最棒的车。他有一辆大概是60或61年的凯迪拉克。棕色的。那辆车真漂亮。他会开着车在街区穿行。我当时并不知道他是个皮条客。但他会开着车到处走，身边带着女孩。

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:38.290
<v Kevin>And one of the things he'd always do was he would-- we'd have the ice cream truck that would roll through the neighborhood. He'd stop and get out, and he'd buy all the kids ice creams. So obviously, the image for us of Robert Charles was like, wow, this guy's really successful, he's got lots of money, and all of us aspired to be like Robert Charles.
<v Kevin>他总是做的一件事是——我们街区会有冰淇淋车经过。他会停下来下车，给所有的孩子买冰淇淋。所以很明显，在我们眼中，罗伯特·查尔斯的形象就是：哇，这个人真的很成功，他有很多钱，我们所有人都渴望成为像罗伯特·查尔斯那样的人。

00:05:38.290 --> 00:06:18.110
<v Kevin>Right outside of Berkeley, on the Oakland border, is a hotel. It's called California Hotel. At that time, back in the '60s, it was, I'm guessing, a 600-room hotel, a really big hotel. Had a ballroom, a nightclub. At that time, Richard Pryor, who was just starting to burst on the national scene, was doing stand-up there. It reminds me of what I've read about what Harlem was like. It was an area where people who were black who were successful would go.
<v Kevin>在伯克利外围，奥克兰的边界上，有一家酒店。叫加州酒店。在60年代那时候，我猜它有600间房，是一家非常大的酒店。有宴会厅，有夜总会。当时，刚开始在全国走红的理查德·普赖尔就在那里表演脱口秀。这让我想起我读到过的关于哈莱姆区的样子。那是一个黑人成功人士会去的地方。

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:46.580
<v Kevin>Well, one of the things that happened was that whole California Hotel and the street San Pablo had become a red-light district. I remember the first time I rode down there, going down San Pablo and seeing all the girls-- dressed, standing on the corners, soliciting to the cars as they walked by, the short dresses, the skimpy outfits, everything.
<v Kevin>嗯，发生的事情之一就是整个加州酒店和圣巴勃罗大街变成了一个红灯区。我记得第一次开车经过那里，沿着圣巴勃罗大道看去，看到所有的女孩们——盛装打扮，站在街角，向过往的车辆招揽生意，穿着短裙、暴露的服装，应有尽有。

00:06:46.580 --> 00:07:15.300
<v Kevin>And every pimp who was ever a pimp in the whole area drove up and down this street. They drove up and down this drill. And at that point, it's really funny, because it's different now. I don't think a pimp would want to be out there now. They sort of want to stay in the background. But then, you wore it like a badge of honor.
<v Kevin>而且在该地区的所有皮条客都会开着车在那条街上反复兜圈。他们在那条“演习线路”上来回行驶。现在想来真是有趣，因为现在情况不同了。我想现在的皮条客不会想在大庭广众之下抛头露面。他们更倾向于隐藏在幕后。但在当时，你把它当作一种荣誉勋章。

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:53.110
<v Kevin>When we originally began going down there, keep in mind, we were catching the bus there. We would just go down there and get the bus. AC Transit. We would get down there, we'd get off the bus, and we would just walk around. We might tease the girls, hey, how you doing honey? What's up? Where's your man at? He'll be back in 15 minutes. Just jiving-- shuckin' and jivin', as they call it-- with the girls. You've got to keep in mind, now, we're going down there, but at the same time, we're going back to school the next day.
<v Kevin>当我们最初开始去那里的时候，要记住，我们当时还是坐公交车去的。我们只是坐公交车去那里。AC公交。我们下车后，就会在那儿四处溜达。我们可能会调侃那些女孩，“嘿，你好吗宝贝？怎么了？你的男人在哪儿？”“他15分钟后回来。”就这样调情——用他们的话说叫“闲扯”——和女孩们聊天。你要记住，虽然我们去那里，但同时我们第二天还要回学校上课。

00:07:53.110 --> 00:08:22.390
<v Kevin>One thing about Keith is that Keith, at some point, made a decision that that was more important to him, to be successful at that, than anything else. He made the step that none of the others were willing to make, and that was to drop out of school. His first thing was that, I've got to get me a car. I've got to get me a car. I've got to have flash. I've got to have something to show.
<v Kevin>关于基思的一点是，基思在某个时刻做出决定，对他来说，在拉皮条上取得成功比任何事情都重要。他迈出了其他人都不愿迈出的一步，那就是辍学。他的第一件事就是，我得弄辆车。我得弄辆车。我得有派头。我得有东西来展示。

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:54.030
<v Kevin>I remember Keith, he couldn't afford a Cadillac. So what he had was a Ford. He had, I think, a Ford Falcon. It was a convertible, though. And here's what he did. He painted it red, bright red. And he had the convertible top taken off-- it was a black convertible top-- and he had a white convertible top put on it. I mean, this was a car that he probably paid $400 for, or something like that. But when he finished it, it looked shiny and new.
<v Kevin>我记得基思，他买不起凯迪拉克。所以他有一辆福特。我想是福特猎鹰（Ford Falcon）。不过是敞篷的。这就是他做的：他把它漆成了红色，鲜红色。他拆掉了敞篷车顶——原本是黑色的——换上了一个白色的敞篷车顶。我的意思是，这辆车他大概只花了400美元左右。但当他折腾完时，它看起来光鲜亮丽，像新的一样。

00:08:54.030 --> 00:09:26.930
<v Kevin>And boy, I'll tell you, he'd be riding up and down there. We'd be in the car with him. We would be riding up and down San Pablo. And he'd be in this little, red Ford, which was unheard of. Nobody even went down there in a Ford, but he was bold and brash enough to do that and do it with confidence. It was almost like he was saying, well, look, this is just my little sporty car. I've got a new Rolls Royce at home.
<v Kevin>天哪，我告诉你，他会开着车在街上转来转去。我们坐在他车里。我们会在圣巴勃罗大道上来回兜风。他开着这辆红色的小福特，这简直闻所未闻。没人会开福特去那儿，但他足够大胆、足够狂妄，并自信地做到了这一点。那感觉就像他在说：“看，这只是我的运动小车。我家里还有辆劳斯莱斯呢。”

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:54.250
<v Kevin>So the next thing that Keith needed to do was-- what every pimp needs-- is he needs a girl. You've got to have a girl. I mean, you can have the cars, you can have the clothes, but you have to have a girl. Keep in mind, too. At that point, the area that we were going was so established, you either knocked-- when they say "knocked," pulled some other man's girl-- or you turned a girl out, which would mean that you would introduce her to that whole lifestyle.
<v Kevin>所以基思接下来的需要就是——每个皮条客都需要的东西——他需要一个女孩。你必须得有个女孩。我的意思是，你可以有车，可以有衣服，但你必须得有个女孩。也要记住一点，当时我们去的那个区域非常成熟，你要么去“敲诈”——所谓敲诈就是抢走别人的女孩——要么就是“开发”一个女孩，这意味着你要把她带入那种生活方式。

00:09:54.250 --> 00:10:42.800
<v Kevin>For Keith and us, it was about turning a girl out. That's how Keith got started. He turned out his girlfriend, who had been his longtime girlfriend anyway. Here's how it's introduced to her. One day, Keith is at his house, and we're all laughing and joking and having a drink or whatever. And he goes into the room and he says to her, you know what, Mark's a little drunk. He wants to go to bed with you. I told him to give me $50 and I'd ask you. She's like, no, I don't want to do like this.
<v Kevin>对于基思和我们来说，关键在于“开发”一个女孩。基思就是这样开始的。他开发了自己的女朋友，反正那也是他交往很久的女友。这就是她被带入的过程：有一天，基思在家里，我们都在大笑、开玩笑、喝酒什么的。他走进房间对她说：“你知道吗？马克喝多了。他想和你上床。我告诉他给我50美元，我就去问你。”她说：“不，我不想这样做。”

00:10:42.800 --> 00:11:22.330
<v Kevin>But suddenly, he's talking her into it. Well, you know, it's only Mark. It's not a big deal. We could use the $50, you know what I mean? We could go do something, go out to dinner, go buy you a dress, or something like that. You take the money. I don't want it. You can go buy yourself something with it. So suddenly, this girl is now saying, well, OK, it's only Mark. I kind of know Mark. He's not a bad guy. OK, I'll do it. Well, you see, once you do that, once you sell yourself for money, it becomes very easy to do it again.
<v Kevin>但很快，他开始说服她。好吧，你知道，那只是马克。没什么大不了的。我们可以用这50美元，你明白吗？我们可以去干点别的，出去吃顿晚餐，给你买件裙子，之类的。你拿着钱。我不想要。你可以去买点东西。所以突然间，这个女孩现在说：“好吧，那只是马克。我认识马克。他不是个坏人。好吧，我做。”你看，一旦你这样做，一旦你为了钱出卖自己，再做第二次就变得非常容易了。

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:35.720
<v Tamar Brott>You're there when he pulls this sting on her. Did you feel any sort of pity? Or what did you feel for her, knowing that a door had been opened for her that she would probably go into?
<v Tamar Brott>当他设下这个圈套时你在场。你有没有感到一丝怜悯？或者作为旁观者，你知道她从此开启了一扇通往不归路的大门，你当时是什么感觉？

00:11:35.720 --> 00:12:03.240
<v Kevin>I thought it was great. I really thought it was great watching this guy work. I didn't think it was great to the extent that some girl was getting turned out. I thought it was great to the extent that all of a sudden, everything that those images that we had sought, that we had aspired to, all of it was suddenly happening. And I was actually having the opportunity to be a part of it.
<v Kevin>我觉得很棒。看这个家伙运作，我真的觉得很棒。我并不是说因为有个女孩被“开发”了而觉得棒。我觉得棒是因为突然间，我们所追求的那些形象，我们所渴望的一切，突然间都实现了。而我竟然有机会亲身参与其中。

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:42.460
<v Kevin>It's one thing to watch Robert Charles and his girls. But it's another thing to go through the evolutionary process to see a pimp, so to speak, being born as well as a prostitute. And that was my image at that time. I was excited. It was like, wow, this man is actually getting ready to do what we've only talked about. He's actually getting ready to really do it. And not only that, he's got a girl who made the first step. So it was exciting. We were all excited.
<v Kevin>看罗伯特·查尔斯和他的女孩们是一回事。但亲眼见证一个皮条客的诞生过程，以及一个妓女的诞生，那是另一回事。这就是我当时的印象。我很兴奋。就像是：哇，这个男人真的要去做我们只在嘴上谈论的事情了。他真的要来真的了。不仅如此，他还有一个已经迈出第一步的女孩。所以这很令人兴奋。我们都很兴奋。

00:12:42.460 --> 00:13:06.300
<v Kevin>And after the fact-- and this is what really, I think, and truly makes for a great pimp-- the way that Keith made her feel afterwards. I remember very clearly that when we heard Mark and her come out of the room, we were kind of giggling, right? And I remember Keith getting very serious. He was very serious. He was like, man, cut that [BLEEP].
<v Kevin>事后——我认为这才是真正让基思成为伟大皮条客的原因——基思在那之后对待她的方式。我清楚地记得，当我们听到马克和她从房间里出来时，我们还在咯咯笑，对吧？我记得基思变得非常严肃。他非常严肃。他说：“伙计，别再在那儿扯淡了。”

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:51.370
<v Kevin>He didn't want her in any way to feel like she had been used. He gave her the money. He talked with her. He hugged her. He kissed her. He actually showed her a lot of love at that particular point. And I think that's one of the most significant things when you're turning a girl out. And she became quite a prostitute, as a matter of fact.
<v Kevin>他不想让她觉得自己被利用了。他给了她钱。他和她交谈。他拥抱她。他亲吻她。他在那个特定的时刻真的给了她很多爱。我认为这就是“开发”女孩时最重要的环节之一。事实上，她后来变成了一名非常称职的妓女。

00:13:51.370 --> 00:14:06.090
<v Tamar Brott>I asked Kevin how Mark knew how to turn out his own girlfriend like that. Kevin just said that's how it had always been done. That's how Iceberg Slim did it, and god knows how many pimps before him. The world of pimping is filled with many of these particular customs and bylaws.
