<v Kevin>I actually used to get a kick out of it. I thought it was funny that she was spirited, like a horse. She was spirited and strong willed. And I actually liked that. But for a ho, that wasn't a very good trait to have. And so guys, they used to give me a hard time about that. They was like, man, if you're coming, leave that bitch at home. It got to that point. You know what I mean? Look, if you're coming, man, leave that bitch at home, man. Because that bitch is just disruptive to the game. It got where they didn't want her around their ho.
<v Kevin>我以前真的很享受这种感觉。我觉得她像马一样有灵性很有趣。她很有灵性，意志坚定。我其实很喜欢那样。但对于一个妓女来说，那不是什么好特质。所以其他人会因此给我找麻烦。他们说：“伙计，你要是来，就把那贱人留在家里。”甚至到了那个地步。你明白我的意思吗？“看，伙计，如果你要来，把那贱人留在家里。因为那贱人对游戏干扰太大了。”最后他们甚至不想让她出现在他们的妓女身边。

00:42:00.250 --> 00:42:12.970
<v Kevin>Believe me, over the years I heard people say, man, that nigger ain't no pimp. You know what I mean? He ain't no pimp, you know what I mean? But I even got that from my friends, my best friends.
<v Kevin>相信我，多年来我听到人们说：“伙计，那个黑鬼根本不是个皮条客。”你明白吗？“他根本不是个皮条客。”但我甚至从我的朋友，最好的朋友那里也听到过。

00:42:12.970 --> 00:42:14.630
<v Tamar Brott>So they were critical of you and the way--
<v Tamar Brott>所以他们批评你和你的方式——

00:42:14.630 --> 00:42:25.230
<v Kevin>Sure.
<v Kevin>当然。

00:42:25.230 --> 00:42:42.740
<v Tamar Brott>So at this point, Kevin's become like the pimp who can't shoot straight. His friends keep berating him for fraternizing with Lois, for never giving her quotas. But most appalling is that Kevin's still dating his childhood sweetheart and buying her expensive gifts, all paid for with Lois' money.
<v Tamar Brott>所以到了这个时候，凯文成了一个“枪法不准”的皮条客。他的朋友们不断斥责他与洛伊斯过于亲近，从不给她定额。但最令人震惊的是，凯文仍然在和他的青梅竹马约会，并买昂贵的礼物给她，所有这些都是用洛伊斯的钱支付的。

00:42:42.740 --> 00:43:20.680
<v Kevin>This is the thing that the guys got on me the worst about. The guys said, look, what you're doing is wrong. Because what I would do-- Lois was probably making, like I said, between $400 and $600 in a day. I would shower this other girl with gifts. I mean, just spent incredible amounts of money on her. Diamond rings, gold necklaces. That was another thing the guys, as I said, used to get mad at me. Because they used to say, man, you're not being true to this ho. You know what I mean? You can't be spending this ho's money on this square girl unless this girl is paying you too, you know what I mean? [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Kevin>这就是让伙计们对我最反感的一点。伙计们说：“看，你做的是错的。”因为我会做的事——洛伊斯可能每天挣400到600美元。我会用礼物把这个女孩宠坏。我是说，真的花了大价钱在她身上。钻石戒指、金项链。正如我所说，这也是伙计们对我发火的另一件事。因为他们过去常说：“伙计，你对这个妓女不忠诚。你明白吗？你不能花这个妓女的钱去养那个清纯女孩，除非那个女孩也付钱给你。”

00:43:20.680 --> 00:43:53.100
<v Kevin>And that was one of the rules of the game. You can't be taking money from a ho and then spend it on a square broad, because then, in a way, you're being a trick, just like the trick who's spending money with your ho. You see? And so they didn't understand. This was like a total contradiction to them. It's like, how you going to be a pimp if she's getting money from tricks and then you turn around and being a trick?
<v Kevin>那是游戏规则之一。你不能拿妓女的钱去养别的清纯女人，因为那样的话，在某种意义上，你就成了个“嫖客”，就像那个花钱在你妓女身上的嫖客一样。你明白吗？所以他们不理解。对他们来说，这完全是自相矛盾的。就像，“如果她从嫖客那里拿钱，而你回过头来自己成了嫖客，你算什么皮条客？”

00:43:53.100 --> 00:44:37.060
<v Kevin>We did have an occasion where Lois did something that was totally unacceptable. And at that time, she had left the house. And she had been gone for maybe a week or something like that. And this was totally unacceptable behavior in any relationship, let alone a pimp-ho relationship. So I'm at home, and I'm handling my business. And after a couple of days-- you stay up a couple of nights and you worry. But after a couple days, you get back to your routine. And you know, what the [BLEEP], it'll work itself out. But I remember her sort of waltzing in the house, and me and the boys is there just kicking it, right?
<v Kevin>我们确实遇到过洛伊斯做了完全不可接受的事情的情况。当时，她离开了家。她大概走了一个星期左右。在任何关系中，这都是完全不可接受的行为，更不用说在皮条客与妓女的关系中了。所以我待在家里处理我的生意。几天后——你会熬夜担心。但几天后，你又回到了日常轨道上。你知道，“管他妈的，事情总会解决的。”但我记得她优雅地走进房子，我和伙计们正在那儿消遣，对吧？

00:44:37.060 --> 00:45:08.010
<v Kevin>And I remember her waltzing in the house like nothing happened. It was like, hey, how's it going? And she comes in, and she's heading to the kitchen. And I remember the guys was there. And so I felt like, to some extent, I had to do something. I had to act. I couldn't let her just arrogantly walk in like that with the guys sitting there. So I remember I wheeled around and I slapped her. I slapped her. And I slapper her hard. I mean, I slapped her real hard.
<v Kevin>我记得她像没事儿一样优雅地走进来。就像是：“嘿，怎么样？”她进来了，朝着厨房走去。我记得那些伙计也在那儿。我觉得在某种程度上，我必须做点什么。我必须采取行动。我不能让她在那些家伙面前那么傲慢地走进来。所以我记得我转身扇了她一巴掌。我扇了她。我狠狠地扇了她一巴掌。

00:45:08.010 --> 00:45:47.940
<v Kevin>And when I slapped her, she fell to the ground. And she just laid there knocked out. She was knocked out. And I'm thinking to myself, jeez. Did she hit her head when she was going down? What is all of this about? So she's laying there. I remember looking around, and I think it was Mark who was there. And I remember, Mark looked at me, and he had this sort of look of disgust. Like, this bitch is just playing you like a [BLEEP] yo-yo.
<v Kevin>当我扇她的时候，她摔倒在地上。她就这样昏死过去了。她昏过去了。我心想，天哪。她倒下去的时候撞到头了吗？这一切到底是怎么回事？她就躺在那儿。我记得环顾四周，我想是马克在那儿。我记得马克看着我，他露出一种厌恶的表情。就像是：“这个贱人简直把你像个溜溜球一样耍。”

00:45:47.940 --> 00:46:19.020
<v Kevin>And I'll tell you something really interesting. Almost without thought, I remember I had this pool stick in my hand. And I just whacked her across her back with this pool stick just about as hard as I could. It broke the pool stick in half. And let me tell you, I've never seen nobody jump up this quick in my life. This girl was like she was passed out. I've never seen nobody jump up so quick in my life.
<v Kevin>我告诉你一件非常有意思的事。几乎没有思考，我记得我手里拿着这根台球杆。我用尽全力在那根台球杆上狠狠地抽在她的背上。它把台球杆打成了两截。让我告诉你，我这辈子从没见过有人跳起来这么快。这个女孩就像昏死过去一样。我这辈子从没见过有人跳起来这么快。

00:46:19.020 --> 00:46:50.730
<v Kevin>This girl jumped straight up off the floor and ran out the back door, through the kitchen, out the back door. I mean, she just jumped. It's almost like she had springs. And I remember Mark saying to me, "Man, that bitch is trying to play you." He said, "Man, that bitch you slapped, she was acting like she was knocked out. She was going to lay there all night if you'd have let her." He said, "Man, that bitch is playing on you." He said, "Kevin, you know what, man? You need to take care of that bitch, because that bitch is straight playing you."
<v Kevin>这个女孩从地板上直挺挺地跳起来，从后门跑了出去，穿过厨房，跑出了后门。我是说，她就像有弹簧一样跳了起来。我记得马克对我说：“伙计，那个贱人在耍你。”他说：“伙计，你刚才扇的那个贱人，她是在装昏。如果你让她装，她能躺在那儿一整晚。”他说：“伙计，那个贱人在耍你。”他说：“凯文，你知道吗？你得好好管教那个贱人，因为她在赤裸裸地耍你。”

00:46:50.730 --> 00:46:58.350
<v Tamar Brott>Would you have hit her if you were alone?
<v Tamar Brott>如果你是一个人，你会打她吗？

00:46:58.350 --> 00:47:23.160
<v Kevin>Initially, yeah, I probably would have. Yeah, I probably would have, only because I would have known that it was in order. It was what was in order. Yeah. It's almost like I had to. And she knew it.
<v Kevin>起初，会的，我可能会。是的，我可能会，只是因为我会知道那是“该做的”。那是规矩。是的。几乎就像我必须这么做。而她也知道。

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:53.440
<v Kevin>What was really funny was that later, she got bored. Because I lived a fairly normal life now, she got bored. But I remember one day, this guy I heard pulling up to my door and knocking on the door. And I look out and it's Lois. And she's got this guy with her. And he's a guy who, actually, I had known, I had seen around, who I knew to be a pimp named Jared.
<v Kevin>真正有趣的是，后来，她厌烦了。因为我现在过着相当正常的生活，她觉得无聊了。但我记得有一天，我听到这家伙开车到我家门口敲门。我向外看，是洛伊斯。她带着这个家伙。他是个我实际上认识的人，我曾在周围见过，我知道他是个叫杰瑞德的皮条客。

00:47:53.440 --> 00:48:22.800
<v Kevin>And Jared says to me, "Look, let me talk to you for a minute." So he comes in and he says, "Look, man. Your ho, she re-chose. She's choosing me. She want to be with me now." He said, "I hope you don't have no problem with none of this, man. I just wanted to come to you man to man and let you know what time it is."
<v Kevin>杰瑞德对我说：“看，让我跟你谈谈。”他走进来，说：“看，伙计。你的妓女，她重选了。她选择了我。她现在想跟我在一起。”他说：“我希望你对此没意见。我只是想以男人对男人的方式来通知你现在的情况。”

00:48:22.800 --> 00:48:49.140
<v Kevin>Now, it's really funny. As dignified and as much a part of the game that I had been, and as much as I had seen, at that point, I said to him, fine. I said, that's fine. I was real dignified. I said, that's fine. She can go. And I remember him saying, "She want to get her clothes." I said, that bitch can't get [BLEEP] out of my mother [BLEEP] house. Period.
<v Kevin>现在，这真的很有趣。尽管我曾经那么有尊严，曾经那么深入地参与游戏，看过那么多场面，但在那一刻，我对他说：“好吧。”我说：“那很好。”我表现得非常有尊严。我说：“那很好。她可以走。”我记得他说：“她想取回她的衣服。”我说：“那个贱人别想从老子的房子里拿走任何一件该死的东西。没门。”

00:48:49.140 --> 00:49:21.790
<v Kevin>And it's really funny because, see, in the name of the game, I shouldn't have did that. I should've said, sure, go ahead. But at that point, even after all the training, after all the schooling, after all the being around, and after understanding how the game goes, at that point, I lost it. And I was like, no. You've got her, but the bitch can't have a [BLEEP] thing from me.
<v Kevin>这真的很有趣，因为看，以游戏的名义，我不该那样做。我应该说：“好的，拿去吧。”但在那一刻，即使经过了所有的训练，经过了所有的教导，经过了所有的耳濡目染，了解了游戏规则之后，在那个时刻，我失控了。我就像是：“不行。你得到了她，但那个贱人别想从老子这儿带走任何一件狗屁东西。”

00:49:21.790 --> 00:50:10.420
<v Kevin>And it was really funny. It taught me something about the game, because when you're in violation of the game, the game will always come back and bite you. But I remember them leaving. And I remember them returning about 15 minutes later with the police. They came in, they took all her clothes, and they left. Now, you know what? They had every right to, because in the name of the game, I should've gave her stuff back anyway. But at that time, I was so angry she was reverting back. Because one of the things she had always said was that she was so glad that she didn't have a traditional pimp-ho relationship, that she didn't no longer want to be a part of that. But what she taught me was that she longed for it. She actually did miss it.
<v Kevin>这真的很有趣。它教会了我一些关于游戏的道理，因为当你违反游戏规则时，游戏总会回来反咬你一口。但我记得他们离开了。我记得大约15分钟后他们带着警察回来了。他们进来，拿走了她所有的衣服，然后离开了。现在，你知道吗？他们有权这么做，因为以游戏的名义，我无论如何都该把她的东西还给她。但在那时，我太愤怒了，因为她竟然想回归过去。因为她曾一直说她很高兴没有那种传统的皮条客与妓女关系，她不再想参与其中。但她教会我的是，她渴望那种生活。她实际上确实想念它。

00:50:10.420 --> 00:50:15.820
<v Tamar Brott>So you think she just lost respect for you as a pimp since you didn't care about any of that?
<v Tamar Brott>所以你认为她只是因为你不关心那些事而对你失去了身为皮条客的尊重？

00:50:15.820 --> 00:51:15.810
<v Kevin>I think so. Yeah, I think so. I think at some point, she needed somebody who held those values that she held higher than what I held them. As I was telling you, I didn't care if she brought $100 home. And I think when she started to realize that I didn't care, that was so much a part of her self-esteem. She always prided herself in her ability to make money that way. And yeah, I think she started to say, "[BLEEP], this [BLEEP], he don't give a [BLEEP]. He don't give a [BLEEP] about me." That my not caring about the money, for me, translated into me not caring about her, to her.
<v Kevin>我想是的。是的，我想是的。我想在某个时刻，她需要一个像她那样看重那些价值观的人，而不是我这样。就像我告诉你的，我不在乎她带不带100美元回家。我认为当她开始意识到我不在乎时，而那又是她自尊心的很大一部分。她总是以自己那种赚钱的能力为傲。是的，我想她开始说：“妈的，这个烂货，他根本不在乎。他根本不在乎我。”我不在乎钱，对我来说，意味着我不在乎她。

00:51:15.810 --> 00:51:19.160
<v Tamar Brott>Were you heartbroken?
<v Tamar Brott>你当时心碎了吗？

00:51:19.160 --> 00:51:32.720
<v Kevin>At the time, yeah. It was tough.
<v Kevin>当时，是的。很难受。

00:51:32.720 --> 00:51:43.450
<v Tamar Brott>After Lois, Kevin had a few other girls. But his heart wasn't in it. He just wasn't a very good pimp, and they knew it too. And eventually, they left him.
<v Tamar Brott>在洛伊斯之后，凯文还有过几个女孩。但他已经无心经营了。他根本不是个好的皮条客，她们也知道。最终，她们都离开了他。

00:51:43.450 --> 00:52:19.900
<v Kevin>For girls would had been around and who had been with guys who were pimps, they would soon find that I didn't feel that need in them, in terms of being able to control them or even having a desire to, in terms of how a raw pimp would handle them. And so as a result, I would either get rid of them or they would get rid of me. One of the two.
<v Kevin>对于那些一直在周围、曾经和皮条客在一起的女孩，她们很快会发现我并没有那种需求，即控制她们的欲望，甚至没有那种原始皮条客对待她们的欲望。因此，结果就是，要么我赶走她们，要么她们离开我。两者必居其一。

00:52:19.900 --> 00:52:39.090
<v Tamar Brott>Finally, after few years, Kevin got out of the game. By then, it was almost the '80s and pimping was dying. The vice cops I spoke to said women's lib killed off the pimps. They couldn't stand it anymore, giving all their money to a man. Kevin said it was a combination of that and crack cocaine that did in the '70s pimp.
<v Tamar Brott>最终，几年后，凯文退出了这场游戏。那时已经快到80年代了，拉皮条行业正在走向没落。我采访的风化案警察说，妇女解放运动终结了皮条客。她们再也无法忍受把所有的钱都交给一个男人了。凯文说这是妇女运动和快克可卡因共同导致了70年代皮条客的终结。

00:52:39.090 --> 00:53:21.010
<v Kevin>Crack cocaine sets you down. When you smoke that stuff, you have no desire to go anywhere. It's almost like you're embalmed. It starts to also trickle down from the pimps to the girls. Because now, their guy, who they had all their self-esteem in, is looking up at all this time, is now sitting in the house. He's just not the same person that he was once before. He's not riding. He's not fun like he used to be. He's not excited anymore. He don't have this whole god complex of being I'm still great and this and that.
<v Kevin>快克可卡因让你彻底堕落。当你抽那玩意儿时，你哪儿都不想去。就像你被防腐处理过了一样。它也开始从皮条客层级渗透到女孩们身上。因为现在，她们引以为傲、寄托所有自尊的男人，正瘫在那儿，坐在这个房子里。他已经不是以前的那个人了。他不出来活动了。他不再像以前那样有趣。他不再兴奋了。他不再有那种上帝情结，觉得自己很了不起之类的了。

00:53:21.010 --> 00:53:42.630
<v Kevin>Every one of these guys that I'm telling you about got involved with crack cocaine. Every single one of them. And eventually, they lost all control. It took out many a pimp. Many a pimp.
<v Kevin>我跟你说的每一个人都沾上了快克可卡因。每一个。最终，他们失去了所有的控制力。这摧毁了许多皮条客。许多皮条客。

00:53:42.630 --> 00:53:49.140
<v Tamar Brott>Did any of them realize as it was happening that this was a downward spiral?
<v Tamar Brott>当事情发生时，他们之中有没有人意识到这是一个下行螺旋？

00:53:49.140 --> 00:54:13.890
<v Kevin>No. Not a single person ever stopped to think, "Well, you know what? Maybe this game is getting old. Maybe it's getting antiquated. Maybe it's time--" they always thought they were coming back. They were coming back. They were always one ho away from coming back, from being on top of the game.
<v Kevin>没有。没有一个人停下来想：“好吧，你知道吗？也许这个游戏太老了。也许它过时了。也许是时候——”他们总以为自己会东山再起。他们会回来的。他们总是距离重回巅峰、统治游戏只差一个妓女。

00:54:13.890 --> 00:54:57.400
<v Kevin>I think the guys who were smart, who aspire to something better, they will always have used the game as a means to getting somewhere else. And that was very clear. But for the guys who were purely in the game for the game itself and felt from day one that the pimping was all they wanted to do, that's all they ever did. They lived it, they'll die it. Simple as that.
<v Kevin>我认为那些聪明、渴望得到更好结果的人，总是会利用这个游戏作为通往其他地方的手段。这一点很清楚。但对于那些纯粹为了游戏本身而入行，从第一天起就觉得拉皮条是他们唯一想做的事情的人来说，那就是他们所做的一切。他们为它而生，也会为它而死。就是这么简单。

00:54:57.400 --> 00:55:36.390
<v Kevin>Still, like I said, it's about the purity and the love and the truth of the game. And that's the thing that-- I always loved that. I always understood the purity by which they approached what they did and their pure love of it. I always admired that, and I still do to this day. I still love the purity of which they loved it, and they studied it. They studied it, and they elevated it to an art form.
<v Kevin>尽管如此，就像我说的，关键在于这种纯粹、爱以及游戏的真实性。这是那一点——我一直很喜欢。我一直理解他们对待所做之事时的纯粹性以及他们对它的纯爱。我一直钦佩这一点，直到今天。我仍然热爱他们对它倾注的那种纯粹的爱，他们研究它。他们研究它，并将其提升为一种艺术形式。

00:55:36.390 --> 00:57:18.680
<v Ira Glass>Kevin, today, makes his living in the straight world creating jewelry for rap stars. He spoke with Tamar Brott in California.
<v Ira Glass>凯文如今在正常社会以制作说唱明星珠宝为生。他在加利福尼亚州接受了塔玛·布罗特的采访。

00:57:18.680 --> 00:57:31.020
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Alix Spiegel and myself, with Nancy Updike and Julie Snyder. Contributing editors, Paul Tough, Jack Hitt, Margy Rochlin, and Consigliere Sarah Vowell. Production help from Jorge Just, Todd Bachmann, and Sylvia Lemus.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目由我和阿利克斯·斯皮格尔制作，南希·厄普代克和朱莉·斯奈德共同参与。特约编辑：保罗·塔夫、杰克·希特、玛吉·罗克林以及顾问萨拉·沃威尔。制作协助：豪尔赫·贾斯特、托德·巴赫曼和西尔维娅·莱姆斯。

00:57:31.020 --> 00:57:32.320
